Kevin Wayne  <killedbyafoo@yahoo.com> wrote:
> If I wanted to play a puzzle game I'd get a puzzle game and play it.

In spite of my extensive post elsewhere in this thread about other
puzzle games which could be shoehorned into a roguelike, I'm
actually inclined to agree with this sentiment.

For me, the point of a puzzle game is to force you to figure out one
of a small number of solutions to a problem within some very
restrictive rules, whereas the point of a roguelike is to model a
great big sprawling environment of (ideally) comparable complexity
to the real world, such that there's generally more than one
entirely different way to do anything. Thus, to embed any puzzle
game in a roguelike sort of misses the point.

NH's puzzly bits are just about OK on this score: there are ways
into the Castle other than by doing the Mastermind thing, you can
cheat at Sokoban if you want to (in which case your replacement
problem becomes how to replenish your depleted luck) or ignore it
completely (in which case the problem becomes where else to get all
the useful stuff from). You might almost consider the maze-shaped
levels to be a puzzle, but, well, hand me that uncursed pick-axe.
